# Reproduce: cargo test --locked -p xai-test-utils --lib
#           (the guard); the race itself needs a git new enough to start auto-maintenance,
#            see the container loop below.
# Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, host git 2.34.1. Container: ubuntu:24.04 + ppa:git-core/ppa,
#       git 2.55.0 -- the same git the GitHub runners reported.
# Commit under test: the commit that adds this transcript (previous HEAD 88903bf7).
# Scope: why `rust check / clippy / test` went red on Linux, and with it why the macOS and
#        Windows platform legs reported "skipped" instead of running.

== the symptom

CI run 36984856317 (commit 88903bf7, main, pushed 2026-10-02T08:35Z):

    rust check / clippy / test           completed failure
    npm package scripts                  completed success
    GUI browser E2E                      completed success
    docs localization / secret scan      completed success
    platform tests (${{ matrix.os }})    completed skipped

The platform legs are `needs: [rust]`, so one red test on Linux hides every macOS and
Windows result. Out of 508 tests in the crate, one failed:

    ---- git::safety::tests::reachability::snapshot_of_the_source_goes_when_the_source_still_holds_it stdout ----
    thread '...' panicked at crates/codegen/xai-fast-worktree/src/git/safety_tests.rs:122:39:
    called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: Os { code: 2, kind: NotFound, message: "No such file or directory" }

The runs around it failed the same job on different tests (`commit::tests::...` in
36972357636), which is what flakiness looks like from the outside. The panic text named no
path, so nothing in CI said which file was missing.

== step 1 -- make the fixture say which file it could not open

`safety_tests.rs:122` is `std::fs::copy(&from, &to).unwrap()` inside the fixture's recursive
`copy_tree`. `unwrap()` there reports the errno and nothing else. The helper now reports the
source, the destination, whether the source still exists and whether the destination's
parent exists.

Then the test binary was run in a loop inside the container (git 2.55.0, the runner's git).
Nothing about CI was needed to reproduce it:

    /t.bin --test-threads=4      # 12 consecutive full runs of the crate's lib tests
    run 1..3  ok
    run 4  FAILED  git::safety::tests::git_dir::lfs_object_beside_the_store_keeps_the_worktree
    run 5..10 ok
    run 11 FAILED  git::safety::tests::reachability::the_worktree_cannot_be_its_own_surviving_repository
    run 12 ok
    -> 2 failures in 12 runs, on two different tests

So the flake is in the repository's own test fixtures, not in the CI image. Which test loses
is just which one happened to be copying at the wrong moment. With the new message, run 7
of a second loop said:

    copy /tmp/.tmpxdZ6CS/source/.git/objects/maintenance.lock -> /tmp/.tmpxdZ6CS/reset-hard/.git/objects/maintenance.lock:
      No such file or directory (os error 2) (source still there: false, dest parent there: true)

A file that was in the directory listing and was gone by the time it was opened, inside
`.git/objects`, named `maintenance.lock`.

== step 2 -- why a lock file appears in a listing at all

`git commit` does not only commit: it schedules git's auto-maintenance in a detached child
process. That child takes `.git/objects/maintenance.lock` and unlinks it when it finishes,
so the lock outlives the `git commit` that spawned it -- the command has already returned and
the test's `run_git` has already asserted success. A fixture that copies or walks `.git`
immediately afterwards races a process nobody is waiting for.

Four git configurations were measured, 15 commits each, with a busy poll for the lock after
every commit (git 2.55.0):

    | configuration                  | maintenance.lock seen |
    |--------------------------------|-----------------------|
    | default                        | 15/15                 |
    | -c maintenance.auto=false      |  0/15                 |
    | -c gc.writeCommitGraph=false   | 15/15                 |
    | -c gc.auto=0                   |  0/15                 |
    | all three                      |  0/15                 |

Every default commit starts the child. Neither task has anything to do in a scratch
repository that holds a handful of commits, so the test helper now starts neither.

`gc.writeCommitGraph=false` is in the table because it was the obvious guess and is wrong:
suppressing the commit-graph task does not stop the child, it only gives it less to do.

== step 3 -- the fix

`xai_test_utils::git::git_command_with_identity` already masks the machine's git
configuration (`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null`, `GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1`) and pins the locale.
Background auto-maintenance is the same class of machine-dependent state, so it is pinned
there too, through `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT` / `GIT_CONFIG_KEY_n` / `GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_n` rather
than `-c`, so it also reaches the sub-commands git runs itself (`git stash` invoking
`git update-index`):

    GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=2  GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=maintenance.auto  GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=false
                        GIT_CONFIG_KEY_1=gc.auto           GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_1=0

That covers every crate that shells out to git through the shared helper. The two other
test-side recursive tree copiers (`xai-grok-workspace/src/session/git_tests.rs`,
`xai-grok-pager/src/test_util.rs`) build their repositories with `git2`, which never spawns
a maintenance child, and are not exposed to this.

The guard is `xai-test-utils`'s own test:
`git::tests::helper_git_does_not_schedule_background_auto_maintenance`. It asserts the
configuration git actually resolves for a command from the helper (version-independent), and
then polls the lock path for 50 ms after a real commit (the symptom).

    $ cargo test --locked -p xai-test-utils --lib
    test git::tests::helper_git_does_not_schedule_background_auto_maintenance ... ok
    test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed

It is load-bearing: with the five `.env(...)` lines deleted,

    test git::tests::helper_git_does_not_schedule_background_auto_maintenance ... FAILED
    git ["config", "--get", "maintenance.auto"] failed:

and with them restored it passes again.

== step 4 -- the loop again, after the fix

Same binary source, same container, same git 2.55.0:

    4-thread:  0 failures in 20 runs
    16-thread: 0 failures in 10 runs

against 2 failures in 12 runs before. The 16-thread arm is well past what CI runs (the
runners have 4 vCPUs).

== what this is NOT evidence about

* It is not a re-run of CI. The green Linux job and the now-unblocked macOS/Windows legs are
  still ahead; `docs/verification/platform-ci-2026-10-02.log` records what the platform legs
  said before this fix.
* It does not explain the other CI failure in this family,
  `commit::tests::committed_edit_keeps_diff_line_backgrounds`
  (`xai-grok-pager-minimal/src/commit_tests.rs:846`, run 36972357636). That test touches no
  git repository; it renders a diff under `Theme::current()` and tripped its own
  "the pinned theme must carry a real insert background" guard. It is in the same
  cross-test-mutates-a-process-global family but has a different fix: the test now holds the
  shared theme lock through the assertions (`20727366`, committed 07:34Z, after that run's
  06:11Z commit), so it should not recur -- re-observing it green in CI is still ahead.
* It does not cover the Windows `exit code 127` in run 36979345879. That was `rg` not being
  where the CI step said it was, and was fixed on its own in 88903bf7.
* The guard asserts the configuration and the absence of the lock. It cannot assert anything
  about a git release old enough never to start the child -- host git 2.34.1 never created
  the lock in the same probe, which is why the configuration half of the assertion exists.
